    {
      "id": "land-regular-42-02",
      "identityNameBase": "Tobias Tumble",
      "characterId": "tobias-tumble",
      "fullSpecies": "Lontra canadensis",
      "title": "A little farther up",
      "platform": "facebook",
      "contentType": "post",
      "status": "candidate",
      "independentLeadReview": "pending",
      "prose": "I came out of the river with a bit of weed caught in the fur under my leg. Rolled onto my back, curled around it and started pulling it loose with my teeth.\n\nThe whole bank began moving uphill.\n\nStraightened out. Dug my feet into the snow. Stopped with my tail hanging over the edge and the weed still attached.\n\nClimbed back up and lay across the slope this time. Got the weed free. Worked through the fur around it, then the other side, without sliding anywhere.\n\nWhen I was finished, I turned downhill and tucked my feet in.",
      "altText": "A river otter lies on its back on sloping snow above a rocky edge, with limbs tucked near its body and its long tapering tail extended.",
      "fictionBoundary": "Exact NPS caption records an otter on its back preening. Snow slope, curled body and grooming anchor the event. Weed, accidental slide, braking, climb, reorientation and deliberate final slide are fiction. No attached weed is visible. No injury, dangerous fall, drowning risk or claim that the animal confuses its limbs. The moving-bank sentence is subjective motion, resolved by the next actions.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/0b7f4117-2eca-4b67-a864-91b02d09d84a",
      "creator": "NPS/Jim Peaco",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "rightsEvidence": {
